-- title:
--   Rademacher sums in l2-sums
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. The cube $\{0,1\}^n$ (Booleans) carries the uniform average $\mathbb E$, the signs $\varepsilon_i=\pm1$, the Walsh characters $w_A(\varepsilon)=\prod_{i\in A}\varepsilon_i$, and the norm $\|f\|_{L_2}=(\mathbb E\|f(\varepsilon)\|^2)^{1/2}$ for $X$-valued functions. Let $\Omega$ be finite.
--
--   1. **Coordinatewise Rademacher sums.** For $x_1,\dots,x_m\in\ell_2(\Omega;X)$,
--   $$
--   \Bigl(\sum_i\varepsilon_ix_i\Bigr)(a)=\sum_i\varepsilon_ix_i(a)\qquad(a\in\Omega).
--   $$
--   So a fixed-dimension cotype-$2$ bound for $X$ passes to $\ell_2(\Omega;X)$ with the same constant, by summing the coordinate inequalities.
